WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.910
Film Courage: You began making movies at three
years old?

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:06.240
Julian Shaw, Filmmaker/Actor: I would say
three years old is when I first started making

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.240
films.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:14.889
I had a VHS camcorder right and I was so inspired
by New Zealand’s Funniest Home Videos which

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:19.569
was obviously the version of the Bob Saget
America’s Funniest Home Videos.

00:00:19.569 --> 00:00:26.079
I would actually write and perform sketches
that we would submit to New Zealand’s Funniest

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:27.079
Home Videos.

00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:32.390
It might be like it’s my birthday and I
would end up falling into the birthday cake

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.640
or we’d be playing cricket and I’d get
hit in the nether region with a tennis ball.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.460
And I was operating the camera and my dad
would be like “Make sure you get this angle.”

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.990
And my sister and my mom would act in these
things.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.080
So that was technically my first directing
and acting.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.560
Film Courage: What has been the longest time
you’ve ever taken a break from filmmaking?

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:57.030
Julian: You know once I got going with it
I just haven’t really stopped, it’s been

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.370
pretty relentless.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:04.350
I got into it in my mid-teens pretty hardcore
and I’ve been going ever since.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.380
I’ve had to re-evaluate where I am in my
career and change parts but I’ve never really

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.380
stopped.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.820
Film Courage: I read in your bio that you
suffered from burnout at one point?

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.909
Julian: Yes…for sure.

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:20.170
What happened was I made the two documentaries.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:26.009
They were one-hour documentaries and each
one of them took four years.

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:27.680
And I kind of did that on my own.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.509
I hadn’t gone to film school.

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:34.280
I had an amazing producer named Jonathan Green
who supported me and they got funding and

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:40.329
we did really well but I just got to a point
where I thought “Man, I’ve gone as far

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:43.299
as I can without some help.”

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:48.090
And that was when I decided to go to film
school which was in my mid-twenties because

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.950
I just felt it was time to get that kind of
feedback.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.700
It was time to study and hone my craft.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.029
I already started to find my voice but I just
needed to work on the technique a bit.

00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:03.500
That was how I got out of the funk that I
was in because I was kind of depressed coming

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.990
off of my second documentary.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.920
Film Courage: I have heard that from other
people to because there is like this let down,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.190
like it’s finished, it’s out there and
then you don’t know how it’s going to

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.630
be received and the excitement of it is over
as well.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.150
Julian: And just money…let’s be honest.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.170
It all comes down to money and financing these
films.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.810
Often it is tough financially along the way
where you can make a lot of money in the film

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:33.450
industry but we know it’s often not at the
front end when you are starting out, so that’s

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.450
tough.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.890
My second film was really challenging.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:44.830
I had some legal issues with it and I also
documentary family members in my film.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.860
It was a film about New Zealander’s obsession
with rugby.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.570
So you might think “Well why is your family
in it?”

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.910
Well the reason is I was documenting my relationship
with my father because I realized that was

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:59.701
the key to why I loved this rugby team from
New Zealand (the All Blacks), that was it.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:04.690
It was my relationship with him and I had
some unresolved issues from that relationship.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.820
So I decided to document that in the film.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.820
My dad and I have never been so close, we
are awesome now.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:17.260
But it was tough at the time because even
though I was telling my truth in the film,

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.540
that wasn’t his truth.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:24.330
He didn’t recognize the character that was
him.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.340
So that was really tough because I went into
it with the best intentions you know.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.860
I’m going to make this truthful documentary.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.090
I’m going to tell my truth.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.650
Well my truth isn’t my subject’s truth.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.140
And I kind of had a breakdown about “Gosh,
what is a documentary?

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.260
Can you make anything that is really truthful?”

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:46.100
I realized how subjected it is even with the
best intentions.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:51.790
And I think that was why I was ready to smash
that mold of documentary making on this film

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.600
USE ME that I just made.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.450
Because I didn’t feel that a pure documentary
was kind of a form that I even believed in

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.230
anymore to be completely honest with you.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:05.250
Film Courage: Yes, I’ve heard that dilemma
from other documentarians where sometimes

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:09.380
a film is never released just because the
quote-un-quote “subject” doesn’t want

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:16.100
to be seen in a certain light even though
even though it’s kind of this raw light,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.459
they want to have it a certain way or look
a certain way.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:20.790
Julian: One hundred percent.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:26.030
And look it’s that thing where you shoot
a hundred hours of footage, it’s 3D, it’s

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.370
in person, it’s the real thing, it’s real
life.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:33.440
And then you see a movie and it’s 2D and
a hundred hours turns into 1 hour and you

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.910
are lying through omission, you are lying
just by cutting things out or just by choosing

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:41.680
to emphasize certain things and you are trying
to craft it into an emotionally satisfying

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:42.680
story.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.920
Now of course you are not trying to mislead
people but there is a sculpting of that material.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.160
I’m saying really obvious things here but
sometimes when the people you’ve portrayed

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.610
in the film see that it is a rude awakening.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:59.220
Film Courage: Sure, I’ve definitely heard
of people where there is tons of footage and

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.010
they can’t do anything with it.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:09.000
Julian: Absolutely and you try and communicate
with your subjects but it is still your interpretation

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.630
as the director and…I don’t know?

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:17.900
How truthful is documentary it is still one
person’s point of view even with the best

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:23.139
intentions of being honest it’s still that
director shaping reality into a story.

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:27.190
Film Courage: What I find interesting is you
had sort of the opposite journey in that most

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.910
people go to film school (Whether they become
jaded or not.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:34.170
Whether they feel it was helpful or not) and
they go onto work or so things in the industry

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.220
or maybe they don’t.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.780
But you had the opposite experience.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:42.920
So did being in class with other people who
were excited about movies reignite the passion

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:43.920
for film?

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.850
Julian: It really did when I went to film
school, it sort of jolted me back to life

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:53.810
and kind of got me out of the funk that I
was in.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.320
It was interesting because I studied directing
at the National Film School in Australia which

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.600
is call AFTRS.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:07.862
What was funny was it also reignited my love
of acting because I had wanted to act when

00:06:07.862 --> 00:06:11.570
I was a kid, I had gone to a performing arts
high school in Australia, I was acting two

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.350
hours a day (that was part of the syllabus).

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:17.230
And then kind of moved away from that to focus
on directing.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:22.150
Then I go to film school and our teacher Samantha
Lang was taking these acting classes and so

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.040
we were up acting for each other for a week.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.460
And at the end of it one fo the director said
“You know I think acting is your calling.”

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:35.960
And then these other directors that I studied
with started casting me in your film so that

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:41.669
was an amazing revelation for me and at that
point it sort of got me back in love with

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:42.669
acting.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:46.800
So being in that environment was so inspiring
on so many levels.

